 Network: cables, routers and servers connected with each other
 Router: A networking device that forwards data packets between computer networks. They know where to send your packets on the internet!
 Switch: Takes a packet and send it to the correct ser ver / client on your network.


Problems with traditional IT approach
• Pay for the rent for the data center
• Pay for power supply, cooling, maintenance
• Adding and replacing hardware takes time
• Scaling is limited
• Hire 24/7 team to monitor the infrastructure
• How to deal with disasters? (earthquake, power shutdown, fire…)
• Can we externalize all this?

Cloud Computing: Zero → Hero

Let's imagine we are building a real application:

Example: An e-commerce website like Amazon/Flipkart
Users open the website → browse products → login → place orders → payment → receive notifications.

We'll use this same example to understand almost every cloud concept.

1. What is Cloud Computing?
Traditional way — Before Cloud

Suppose you start an e-commerce company.

You need:

Physical servers
Hard disks/SSD
RAM
Networking equipment
Load balancers
Firewalls
Data center
Electricity
Cooling
Backup systems
System administrators

You purchase everything yourself.

             Your Company
                  |
          ┌───────┴────────┐
          |   Data Center  |
          |                |
       Server 1         Server 2
          |                |
       Database         Application

If 100 users use your application, maybe 1 server is enough.

But suppose suddenly 1 lakh users visit because of a sale.

You need more servers.

Buying physical servers takes time and money.

2. Cloud Computing

Instead of buying physical infrastructure, you rent computing resources from a cloud provider.

For example:

Amazon Web Services
Microsoft Azure
Google Cloud

With AWS:

             Internet
                 |
                 ↓
              AWS Cloud
                 |
       ┌─────────┼─────────┐
       ↓         ↓         ↓
     EC2        S3        RDS
   Servers    Storage    Database

You don't physically own those servers.

You use them when required and pay according to the service/usage model.

That's the fundamental idea of cloud computing.

3. Simple Real-Life Example

Think about electricity.

Without electricity grid

You would need:

Buy Generator
      ↓
Buy Fuel
      ↓
Maintain Generator
      ↓
Repair Generator
      ↓
Generate Electricity
With electricity provider

You simply:

Electricity Provider
        ↓
      Power
        ↓
      You
        ↓
   Pay for usage

Cloud works similarly.

Instead of:

"I need to buy 10 servers."

You say:

"I need 10 virtual servers."

The cloud provider provides them.

4. What exactly does "Cloud" contain?

Cloud isn't one thing.

It contains many types of resources.

For AWS, you'll eventually learn:

AWS
│
├── Compute
│   └── EC2
│
├── Networking
│   ├── VPC
│   ├── Subnets
│   ├── Route Tables
│   ├── Internet Gateway
│   └── Load Balancer
│
├── Storage
│   ├── S3
│   └── EBS
│
├── Database
│   ├── RDS
│   └── DynamoDB
│
├── Security
│   ├── IAM
│   └── Security Groups
│
├── DNS
│   └── Route 53
│
├── Monitoring
│   └── CloudWatch
│
└── Messaging
    ├── SNS
    └── SQS

Don't try to memorize everything initially.

Understand why each service exists.

5. What is a Server?

Before understanding EC2, understand a server.

Suppose your application is written in Java:

Java Application
       ↓
      JVM
       ↓
 Operating System
       ↓
    Hardware

The hardware might have:

CPU
RAM
Storage
Network

A server is essentially a computer that provides services to other computers/users.

6. What is a Virtual Machine?

Suppose you have one physical server:

Physical Server
CPU: 16 cores
RAM: 64 GB

You can divide it into multiple virtual machines:

Physical Server
│
├── VM1 → 4 CPU / 16 GB RAM
├── VM2 → 4 CPU / 16 GB RAM
├── VM3 → 4 CPU / 16 GB RAM
└── VM4 → 4 CPU / 16 GB RAM

Each VM behaves approximately like an independent computer.

Cloud providers use virtualization heavily to provide computing resources.

7. AWS EC2

EC2 = Elastic Compute Cloud

Think:

EC2 = Virtual Server in AWS

For example, your e-commerce application needs a Linux server.

You create:

EC2 Instance
│
├── Ubuntu/Amazon Linux
├── CPU
├── RAM
├── Disk
└── Network

Then deploy your application.

User
 ↓
Internet
 ↓
EC2
 ↓
Your Application

This is one of the most important concepts for a DevOps engineer.

8. What is Storage?

Your application needs to store files.

For example:

profile.jpg
product1.jpg
product2.jpg
invoice.pdf
backup.zip

You don't necessarily want these files sitting on your application server.

AWS provides S3.

S3 = Simple Storage Service

Think of S3 as massive cloud object storage.

                 S3
                  |
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
       ↓          ↓          ↓
   image.jpg   invoice.pdf  backup.zip

Real example:

A user uploads a profile picture:

User
 ↓
Website
 ↓
Application
 ↓
S3
 ↓
profile.jpg
9. What is a Database?

Your application needs to store structured information:

Users
Products
Orders
Payments
Addresses

Example:

Users Table

ID     Name       Email
1      Rahul      rahul@gmail.com
2      Priya      priya@gmail.com

AWS provides managed database services such as RDS.

Instead of manually installing and maintaining a database server, you can use an AWS-managed database service.

Application
     |
     ↓
    RDS
     |
 ┌───┴────┐
 |        |
Users    Orders
10. EC2 vs S3 vs RDS

This is extremely important.

Service	Purpose	Example
EC2	Compute/server	Run application
S3	Object storage	Store images/videos/files
RDS	Relational database	Store users/orders

Think:

EC2 = Computer
S3  = File storage
RDS = Database
11. What happens when 1 server isn't enough?

Suppose:

100 users
 ↓
EC2

Works perfectly.

Then your company becomes popular:

100,000 users
       ↓
      ???

One server may not handle the traffic.

So you add multiple servers.

              Users
                |
        ┌───────┴───────┐
        ↓               ↓
      EC2-1           EC2-2

Now you have another problem.

Which server should receive each request?

That's where a Load Balancer comes in.

12. Load Balancer
                Users
                  |
                  ↓
           Load Balancer
             /        \
            /          \
           ↓            ↓
        EC2-1         EC2-2

The load balancer distributes traffic among servers.

For example:

Request 1 → EC2-1
Request 2 → EC2-2
Request 3 → EC2-1
Request 4 → EC2-2

In AWS, you'll commonly encounter Elastic Load Balancing, including Application Load Balancer (ALB).

13. What if traffic suddenly increases?

Suppose your e-commerce company announces:

Big Billion Day / Festival Sale

Normal traffic:

10,000 users

During sale:

1,000,000 users

You don't want to manually create 50 servers.

This is where Auto Scaling becomes important.

Normal traffic

        Load Balancer
             |
        ┌────┴────┐
       EC2       EC2

Traffic increases:

             Load Balancer
                  |
       ┌──────────┼──────────┐
      EC2        EC2        EC2
                              ↓
                            EC2

Traffic decreases:

             Load Balancer
                  |
             ┌────┴────┐
            EC2       EC2

AWS can automatically adjust capacity according to configured scaling policies.

14. What is Networking?

Now imagine your application has:

Internet
   ↓
Load Balancer
   ↓
Application Servers
   ↓
Database

You don't want your database directly accessible from the public internet.

You need a network architecture.

That's where VPC comes in.

15. VPC
VPC = Virtual Private Cloud

Think of it as:

Your private network inside AWS.

Example:

                 Internet
                    |
                    ↓
             Internet Gateway
                    |
              ┌─────┴─────┐
              │    VPC    │
              │           │
              │ Public    │
              │ Subnet    │
              │    ↓      │
              │   ALB     │
              │    ↓      │
              │ Private   │
              │ Subnet    │
              │   EC2     │
              │    ↓      │
              │ RDS       │
              └───────────┘

This is where your AWS networking journey really starts.

16. Public vs Private Subnet
Public subnet

Resources can have a route toward the internet through the VPC's internet gateway.

Example:

Internet
   ↓
Internet Gateway
   ↓
Public Subnet
   ↓
Load Balancer
Private subnet

Used for resources that shouldn't be directly reachable from the public internet.

Example:

Application
     ↓
Private Subnet
     ↓
Database

A common architecture is:

Internet
   ↓
ALB
   ↓
EC2
   ↓
RDS

with the application and database placed according to security/networking requirements, often using private subnets for backend/database tiers.

17. DNS — Route 53

Users don't want to type:

13.234.56.78

They want:

www.myshop.com

DNS translates a domain name into the appropriate destination.

www.myshop.com
       ↓
      DNS
       ↓
Load Balancer
       ↓
EC2

AWS's DNS service is Route 53.

18. Security — IAM

Now ask:

Who is allowed to create EC2?

Who can delete S3 buckets?

Who can access RDS?

AWS uses IAM — Identity and Access Management.

For example:

Developer
   ↓
IAM Role
   ↓
Allowed:
  EC2 ✓
  S3 ✓

Not allowed:
  IAM administration ✗

A major cloud security principle is:

Give only the permissions required to perform the job.

This is called least privilege.

19. Monitoring

Your application is running.

But how do you know:

Is CPU high?
Is the server down?
Are requests failing?
Is disk usage increasing?
Is the application healthy?

You need monitoring.

AWS provides CloudWatch.

EC2
 |
 ├── CPU metrics
 ├── Network metrics
 ├── Logs
 └── Alarms
          ↓
      CloudWatch

Example:

CPU > 80%
    ↓
CloudWatch Alarm
    ↓
Notification / scaling action
20. Notifications

Suppose your database has a problem.

You want:

CloudWatch
    ↓
Alarm
    ↓
SNS
    ↓
Email / notification

SNS = Simple Notification Service

So you'll see how AWS services start connecting together.

21. Now Build the Complete Architecture

Let's combine everything.

Imagine our e-commerce application:

                         USERS
                           |
                           ↓
                      Route 53
                           |
                           ↓
                       Internet
                           |
                           ↓
                ┌──────────────────┐
                │   Load Balancer  │
                │       ALB        │
                └────────┬─────────┘
                         |
             ┌───────────┴───────────┐
             ↓                       ↓
          EC2-1                   EC2-2
             |                       |
             └───────────┬───────────┘
                         |
                         ↓
                    Application
                         |
             ┌───────────┴───────────┐
             ↓                       ↓
            S3                      RDS
        Product images          Users/Orders

And:

EC2 / RDS / ALB
      ↓
     VPC
      ↓
Security Groups + IAM
      ↓
CloudWatch
      ↓
SNS

Now you're starting to think like a cloud engineer.

22. What does "Elastic" mean?

You'll hear this word everywhere in AWS.

Elasticity means the ability to increase or decrease resources according to demand.

Example:

Normal:
2 EC2 instances

High traffic:
10 EC2 instances

Traffic decreases:
2 EC2 instances

That's elasticity.

23. Scalability

Scalability means the system can handle increasing workload by increasing capacity.

Two basic approaches:

Vertical scaling

Make one server bigger:

4 CPU / 8 GB RAM
        ↓
8 CPU / 16 GB RAM
Horizontal scaling

Add more servers:

EC2
 ↓
EC2 + EC2
 ↓
EC2 + EC2 + EC2 + EC2

Cloud architectures commonly make heavy use of horizontal scaling.

24. High Availability

What if EC2-1 crashes?

Bad architecture:

Users
  ↓
EC2-1 ❌

Better:

             Load Balancer
              /         \
             ↓           ↓
          EC2-1        EC2-2
             ❌           ✓

Users can continue being served by healthy capacity.

For higher resilience, AWS resources can also be distributed across Availability Zones.

25. Region

AWS has geographical locations called Regions.

For example:

AWS Region
     |
     ├── Availability Zone A
     ├── Availability Zone B
     └── Availability Zone C

A Region is a geographical area containing multiple Availability Zones.

For an Indian deployment, you might encounter the Asia Pacific (Mumbai) Region.

26. Availability Zone

An Availability Zone (AZ) is an isolated location within an AWS Region.

For example:

Mumbai Region
│
├── AZ-1
│    ├── EC2
│    └── RDS
│
├── AZ-2
│    ├── EC2
│    └── RDS
│
└── AZ-3
     └── ...

Why multiple AZs?

Because if one AZ has an outage, your architecture can potentially continue operating using another AZ.

27. Cloud Service Models

This is an important interview topic.

IaaS

Infrastructure as a Service

You manage more of the infrastructure.

Example:

EC2

You generally manage:

Application
Runtime
OS
Configuration

AWS manages the underlying physical infrastructure.

PaaS

Platform as a Service

The cloud provider manages more of the platform.

Conceptually:

You
 ↓
Application
 ↓
Platform
 ↓
Infrastructure

You focus more on your application rather than managing servers.

SaaS

Software as a Service

You simply use the software.

Examples include cloud-hosted applications such as:

Gmail
Microsoft 365
Salesforce

You aren't managing the underlying servers.

28. The Responsibility Model

One of the biggest cloud concepts:

AWS manages some things; you manage other things.

For example:

              AWS
               |
       Physical Hardware
       Data Centers
       Networking
       Infrastructure
               |
        ----------------
               |
             YOU
               |
       Application
       Configuration
       Data
       IAM permissions
       OS (for many IaaS services)

The exact division depends on the AWS service.

This is called the Shared Responsibility Model.


29. Why companies use Cloud

Suppose you're launching a startup.

Traditional:

Buy servers
Buy networking
Build data center
Hire infrastructure team
Maintain hardware

Cloud:

Create resources
      ↓
Deploy application
      ↓
Scale when required
      ↓
Pay for resources

Common cloud advantages include:

On-demand resources
Elasticity
Global infrastructure
Automation
Reduced need for upfront hardware investment
Managed services
Disaster recovery options
Faster deployment
29. Why companies use Clo
